faq:
  - q: "What is included in a total compensation package?"
    a: "Total compensation (TC) typically includes: base salary (guaranteed, paid bi-weekly or monthly), annual bonus or target bonus (percentage of base, variable), equity (RSUs, stock options, or profit sharing — vesting over 4 years typically), signing bonus (one-time, often with a clawback if you leave within 12–24 months), and benefits (health insurance, 401k match, PTO, remote stipend). For mid-to-senior roles, equity can represent 30–80% of total annual compensation at tech companies."
  - q: "How do you compare two job offers with different compensation structures?"
    a: "Convert both to annual total compensation (TC) on an apples-to-apples basis: base + target annual bonus + annualized equity value + annualized signing bonus. Example: Offer A has $180K base, $30K annual bonus (target), and $200K RSUs vesting over 4 years ($50K/year). TC = $260K/year. Compare this against Offer B using the same formula. Do not compare base salaries alone — this is where most professionals make their biggest offer evaluation mistakes."
  - q: "What is the difference between RSUs and stock options?"
    a: "RSUs (Restricted Stock Units): you receive actual shares on a vesting schedule. Value is the market price at vesting — no purchase required. Lower risk, lower potential upside. Most common at large, public companies. Stock options: you receive the right to purchase shares at a set strike price. Value is the difference between the market price and strike price at exercise. Higher risk (can be worthless if stock falls below strike), higher potential upside. Common at startups and pre-IPO companies."
  - q: "What is a signing bonus and should I negotiate it?"
    a: "A signing bonus is a one-time cash payment made at or shortly after your start date, usually to compensate for unvested equity you are leaving behind or to make the offer more competitive without changing the recurring compensation structure. It almost always comes with a clawback clause — if you leave within 12–24 months, you must return a prorated portion. Yes, negotiate it — signing bonuses are often easier to increase than base salary because they are one-time costs for the employer."
  - q: "How do I evaluate equity at a startup?"
    a: "Four questions: (1) What percentage of the company do these options/shares represent? (2) What is the current valuation and your strike price? (3) What is the vesting schedule and cliff? (4) What are the liquidity prospects — when might there be an IPO or acquisition? Startup equity is illiquid and speculative. Weight it accordingly. Do not make career decisions primarily on startup equity unless you have done the analysis on realistic scenarios."
  - q: "What benefits are negotiable beyond salary?"
    a: "More than most candidates realize: signing bonus (almost always negotiable), title (sometimes negotiable without changing the budget), start date (often flexible), remote work arrangement (negotiable at many companies even when not advertised as such), first-year performance review timeline (some companies will agree to a 6-month review instead of 12-month), professional development budget, and additional PTO days."
